# Agent Instructions
Describe this project's purpose, implementation boundaries, and local rules here. Adapt this template; do not leave placeholders as active instructions.

## Workflow source
During setup, replace these alternatives with one selected mode and an exact Markdown link to the project’s configuration and applicable local owners. Adapt docs/workflow.toml to the actual documentation folder; verify that link and its relative project root. Remove unused examples.

- Unchanged pinned source: use the complete `project-workflows/` package at the parent gitlink's exact shared revision. Read this project’s [configuration](docs/workflow.toml) with `project.root = ".."` and `adoption.mode = "shared"`. Keep the package detached and unchanged; no project branch is required.
- Existing adapted adoption: use the declared `project-workflows/workflow.toml` and project branch, normally with root `..`. Preserve its source/layout until a separate migration is authorized.
- Global-only: load the installed workflow skills by name. If this project uses configuration, link its actual file here, normally [docs/workflow.toml](docs/workflow.toml), and resolve `project.root` from that file’s folder. Do not read the global installation's config as this project's config or create a submodule/branch.

Read the project's declared policies and applicable local workflow notes before shared defaults. An optional `[workflows]` table in the selected config maps skill names to project-relative Markdown files; load the entry for the skill being used. Add only actual project procedures and useful QA/context, with links to their standing policy owners. Values are file references, never commands, and confer no new policy adoption or action permission. Existing directly declared guidance still applies; unresolved contradictions need human resolution. Report unavailable required skills or context. General/service skills remain optional unless adopted. Existing pinned projects keep their branches and pins; the canonical maintenance installation uses shared main. Update the selected mode under `update-project-workflows`; publication remains separate.

## Agent policies
If this project explicitly adopts the agent-policy system, link its actual project-owned `policy.index.md` here (normally `docs/agent-policies/policy.index.md`) and declare any custom location. Remove this conditional text when adapting the template. Use the selected workflow source’s `create-agent-policy`, `edit-agent-policy`, `review-agent-policies`, `adopt-agent-policies` and `retire-agent-policy` for their actual tasks. The kernel owns obligations and the policy/skill distinction; skills supply procedures under it. Only explicitly adopted policies govern agents in this checkout; saving proposals and updating packages do not adopt them. Keep human governance and shared-branch acceptance separate, and treat dependencies as prerequisites rather than an override hierarchy. Preserve existing legacy policies until their replacement is explicitly adopted. If the system is not selected, retain the existing policy routing instead of creating an empty scaffold.

## Documentation
README explains the project and how to start. TODO indexes active work. Current policies define standing guidance; spike pairs hold scoped reasoning and work state; scratch is provisional; archives preserve history. Actual project documents live in this project, outside any workflow collection. Use `capture-for-later` for individual scratch notes when saving a possibility or deliberately parking a subject. A new note uses only `status: captured` or `status: parked` unless local conventions differ; one sentence is enough. Keep known reasoning and optional revisit conditions without inventing them. Capture does not create tasks or a spike; `run-project-spike` runs active bounded workstreams. Preserve existing notes and legacy lists rather than migrating them during setup.

## Contribution coordination
Declare the project's chosen coordination route and existing review records here. Absent or disabled coordination uses local project records without hosted work items, service setup or publication. If a forge is selected, identify its target repository and platform tooling setup/use source/version; keep agent-access credentials with that tooling owner and CI secrets in the forge's settings. Use `coordinate-project-contribution` from the selected workflow source. Each spike belongs to one agent/thread; independent agents use separate spikes linked to the shared outcome. Humans mediate collaboration agreements. A branch may host many spikes/issues and several eventual change requests, with Commit Work keeping atomic scope traceable. Personal editor/agent choices remain optional, and unsupported agent operations can be handed to a human. Do not infer authority to post, assign, publish or integrate from installed tools.

If board/lifecycle setup is selected, use the setup procedure's optional route and point to this project's maintained profile, transition ownership and verification record. Preserve its stage meanings, independent blocked signals and fresh-close guard; name any pending manual native-settings check. Local use requires none of this board configuration.

## Validation and Git
Record the actual validation commands and commit/review policy. Preserve concurrent work. Review scoped diffs before committing; never include private configuration or credentials. Publishing and remote actions follow the user's authorization and repository policy.

## Markdown style
Keep each prose paragraph or comment paragraph on one line and let editors wrap it. Avoid reflow-only edits. Preserve meaningful line breaks, existing local conventions, and explicit formatter requirements.
